    "standardized_narrative": "The Syndicate reported an overall gross prior year reserve release of GBP 67.7 million in 2021. This was primarily driven by releases related to 2020 COVID-19 losses across the Insurance and Reinsurance segments, and favourable claims development in various classes including Cargo, Marine Liability, Commercial Property, War, Financial Lines, Credit, Cat NA, and Cat International. However, these favourable movements were partially offset by significant adverse development of GBP 94.0 million in Discontinued lines, particularly in US trucking liability (GBP 33.0 million) and International Casualty (GBP 38.0 million), as well as other Casualty classes, due to adverse experience, changes in development patterns, and prior loss ratios.",
    "raw_causal_phrases": [
      "The Syndicate's claims ratio improved to 59% (2020: 76%), with the main difference being the initial COVID losses recognised in 2020.",
      "The net claims ratio has reduced by 12%, largely driven by the absence of COVID losses in 2021.",
      "Reserve releases of GBP 23.0 million in 2021 compare favourably to 2020 which saw a strengthening of GBP 12.0 million. Reserve releases were driven by favourable performance in a host of classes, including Cargo, Marine Liability, Commercial Property (driven by prior year cat releases), War, Financial Lines, and Credit.",
      "The claims ratio of 58% reduced by 40 percentage points compared with 2020 driven by COVID.",
      "Reinsurance division benefitted from GBP 24.0 million of net reserve releases in 2021 (driven by favourable performance in Cat NA and Cat International) compared to a strengthening of GBP 17.0 million in 2020.",
      "The net claims ratio has deteriorated by 145%, largely driven by reserve strengthening. Adverse reserve development totalled GBP 94.0 million in 2021 compared to releases of GBP 14.0 million in 2020. The strengthening is predominantly driven by US trucking liability (GBP 33.0 million) and International Casualty (GBP 38.0 million) classes, which saw significant deterioration in the year, driven by adverse experience, changes to the development pattern and prior loss ratios. Further deteriorations include the Contractors sub-class of the US Casualty book, Aviation, plus other P&C UK classes. These are partially offset by a release in Pro Rata."
